{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"gotchas","title":"Common Gotchas","group":"Modern JS & Best Practices","order":61,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The famous weird parts of JavaScript — each one is logical once you know the rule behind it.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-transform\"></i>Coercion surprises</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>console.log([] == ![], \"b\" + \"a\" + +\"a\" + \"a\");\nconsole.log(null &gt;= 0, null &gt; 0, null == 0);\nconsole.log([1, 2, 3] + [4, 5], typeof (1 + \"1\" - 1));\nconsole.log(Math.max() &lt; Math.min(), 0.1 * 3, 9007199254740992 + 1);\nconsole.log(\"10\" &gt; \"9\", 10 &gt; \"9\", [10, 9, 1].sort());</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>true baNaNa\ntrue false false\n1,2,34,5 number\ntrue 0.30000000000000004 9007199254740992\nfalse true [ 1, 10, 9 ]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Expression</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>[] == ![]</code> → <code>true</code></td><td><code>![]</code> is <code>false</code>; <code>[] == false</code> → <code>\"\" == 0</code> → <code>0 == 0</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>'b' + 'a' + +'a' + 'a'</code></td><td><code>+'a'</code> is <code>NaN</code>, concatenated as text</td></tr><tr><td><code>null &gt;= 0</code> but not <code>null == 0</code></td><td>Relational operators convert <code>null</code> to <code>0</code>; <code>==</code> only equates <code>null</code> with <code>undefined</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Math.max() &lt; Math.min()</code></td><td><code>-Infinity &lt; Infinity</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>'10' &gt; '9'</code> is <code>false</code></td><td>Two strings compare character by character</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code-minus\"></i>Syntax and semantics traps</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>// 1. Automatic semicolon insertion\nconst a = 1\nconst b = a\n;[1, 2].forEach((n) =&gt; console.log(\"ASI needs the leading ;\", n))\n\n// 2. typeof of a TDZ variable throws (unlike undeclared)\ntry { typeof later; } catch (e) { console.log(e.name); }\nlet later = 1;\n\n// 3. array holes and length\nconst arr = [1, , 3];\nconsole.log(arr.length, arr.map((x) =&gt; x * 2), 1 in arr);\n\n// 4. delete on arrays leaves holes\nconst nums = [1, 2, 3];\ndelete nums[1];\nconsole.log(nums, nums.length);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>ASI needs the leading ; 1\nASI needs the leading ; 2\nReferenceError\n3 [ 2, &lt;1 empty item&gt;, 6 ] false\n[ 1, &lt;1 empty item&gt;, 3 ] 3</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-number\"></i>Numbers and dates</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>console.log(parseInt(\"08\"), parseInt(0.0000005), (0.1).toFixed(20));\nconsole.log(new Date(2024, 1, 30).getDate(), new Date(2024, 0, 1).getMonth());\nconsole.log(typeof NaN, [NaN].indexOf(NaN), Number.MIN_VALUE &gt; 0);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>8 5 0.10000000000000000555\n1 0\nnumber -1 true</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>parseInt(0.0000005)</code> is <code>5</code> because the number is first converted to the string <code>\"5e-7\"</code>. Feb 30 rolls over to March 1. <code>Number.MIN_VALUE</code> is the smallest <em>positive</em> double, not the most negative number.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Objects, this and references</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const key1 = {}, key2 = {};\nconst lookup = {};\nlookup[key1] = \"first\";\nlookup[key2] = \"second\";                 // both become \"[object Object]\"\nconsole.log(lookup[key1]);\n\nconst counter = { n: 0, inc: () =&gt; this?.n };\nconsole.log(counter.inc());               // arrow method: no own this\n\nconst defaults = { tags: [] };\nconst a = { ...defaults }, b = { ...defaults };\na.tags.push(\"shared!\");\nconsole.log(b.tags);                      // shallow copy shares the array</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>second\nundefined\n[ 'shared!' ]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock-exclamation\"></i>Async traps</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const results = [];\n[1, 2, 3].forEach(async (n) =&gt; {\n  await null;\n  results.push(n);\n});\nconsole.log(\"right after forEach:\", results);   // nothing awaited it\nawait null; await null;\nconsole.log(\"a bit later:\", results);\n\nconst p = new Promise((resolve) =&gt; resolve(1));\np.then((v) =&gt; console.log(\"then with\", v));\nconsole.log(\"sync line runs first\");</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>right after forEach: []\na bit later: [ 1, 2, 3 ]\nsync line runs first\nthen with 1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Use <code>===</code>, <code>const</code>, <code>Number.isNaN</code>, explicit comparators for <code>sort</code>, <code>Map</code> for object keys, <code>for…of</code>/<code>Promise.all</code> with <code>await</code>, and a linter (ESLint) that flags most of these.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Rely on ASI when a line starts with <code>(</code>, <code>[</code> or a backtick template; <code>for…in</code> on arrays; <code>delete</code> on arrays; arrow functions as object methods.</p></div></div></section>"}